WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.630
If you want to hear how a character from Unbreakable,
Split and Glass also appears in The Village,

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:06.620
then stick around to the end of this video.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:11.040
[INTRO]

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.330
Welcome to Things You Missed, today I'll be
covering one of my favorite movies of all

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:15.670
time, The Village.

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:19.020
If you haven't seen it, I recommend you watch
it first because you definitely don't want

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.510
to spoil this one for yourself.

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:24.119
This thing is such a masterpiece and in the
broadest sense, it's a story about the dangers

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:25.449
of sheltering oneself.

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:28.480
The village elders were each scarred by the
horrible things that happened in the outside

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.070
world, so they created the isolated village
in the Covington Woods to keep themselves

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.750
safe from the heartbreak they endured in modern
society.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.950
But after some time, their sheltering of themselves
kept leading them to then back to that same

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:38.950
heartbreak.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.440
They were blind to the fact that their new
society was becoming the very reason for their

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:46.020
suffering, and it would ironically take someone
who is literally blind to help them see the

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.020
solution.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.379
So in today's episode, I'm also giving the
gift of sight, so that you can see a few Things

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:52.379
You Missed.

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:54.640
[Things You Missed]

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.230
The opening scene is the perfect example of
how backwards the state of affairs in the

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.230
Village has become.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.820
The entire community mourns the death of a
young boy, the son of one of the village elders.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.100
We later find out that the reason for his
death is because the village lacks any kind

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.979
of medicine, and the elders have sworn an
oath never to leave the confines of the village.

00:01:09.979 --> 00:01:13.569
A clue about the secret held by the elders
can be found very close to the beginning of

00:01:13.569 --> 00:01:14.569
the movie.

00:01:14.569 --> 00:01:19.409
"You may run from sorrow as we have.

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:21.929
Sorrow will find you.”

00:01:21.929 --> 00:01:23.969
Nobody is allowed to step foot beyond the
barrier.

00:01:23.969 --> 00:01:25.920
They enforce this rule through the use of
legends.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.490
The legends of the monsters in the woods surrounding
the village draw inspiration from the idea

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.880
of legends in real life.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.200
All throughout history, parents have made
up and passed down stories to get their children

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.200
to behave.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.350
In this case, Those We Do Not Speak Of from
The Village are very similar to the monsters

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.780
that ancient cartographers used to use to
discourage sailors from exploring unknown

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:42.979
areas of the seas.

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:46.499
We even see a similar kind of warning painted
on a rock at the edge of the village.

00:01:46.499 --> 00:01:48.359
Some of the legends are based on real things.

00:01:48.359 --> 00:01:52.539
The idea of the creatures in the woods was
spawned by actual rumors in the area…

00:01:52.539 --> 00:01:58.849
"There did exist rumors of creatures in these
woods, it is in one of the history books I

00:01:58.849 --> 00:02:00.049
used to teach in the towns..."

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:03.520
...and the practice of marking the doors with
red paint is pulled from the religious story

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.149
of Passover, where the Israelites homes where
marked with lamb’s blood thus were passed

00:02:07.149 --> 00:02:09.030
over by the plague of death of the first born.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:12.340
There are other psychological tactics employed
to keep people in the village through the

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.340
use of fear.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.430
They've gone to great lengths to cover the
village in the color yellow, which they call

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:17.430
the protective color.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.459
They rid the village of any reds, because
the settlement is placed in the center of

00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:22.319
a large outgrowth of red berries.

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:25.540
By conditioning the young to associate the
color red with the monsters, they create a

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.980
sense of danger for anyone who leaves the
village.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.770
That's why Ivy is a perfect hero.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.400
Ivy is blind, and therefore, will not be scared
by the color red.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.239
There is a running theme in director M Night
Shyamalan's movies where those who are impaired

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:38.340
are stronger in some unforeseen way.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.599
We see it in Elijah and Kevin from the Eastrail
Trilogy, the Hess family in Signs and Nana

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:44.010
and Pop Pop from The Visit.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:48.159
There's also a strong link between The Village
and Shyamalan's 2013 film After Earth.

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:51.690
In The Village Luscious theorizes that the
creatures sense emotion and fear, and later

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:55.140
Ivy believes she can pass through the woods
because she has no fear.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.080
In After Earth, there are these monsters called
Ursas, who like Ivy are blind, but are able

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.379
to detect pheromones that the human body dispels
when experiencing fear.

00:03:02.379 --> 00:03:06.590
But perhaps the story that most closely ties
in with The Village is the story from Unbreakable,

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:07.670
Split and Glass.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.049
Each has three main characters.

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:13.299
In The Village, we have Noah, who is mentally
ill but physically strong; Ivy, who is physically

00:03:13.299 --> 00:03:17.780
impaired by her blindness, but mentally strong;
and Lucius, who is physically strong and mentally

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:18.780
strong.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.590
The characters in the Eastrail trilogy fill
the same roles: Kevin is mentally ill but

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:26.280
physically strong, Elijah is physically broken
but mentally a genius, and David Dunn is mentally

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:27.550
and physically strong.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:31.799
Lucius and David Dunn even share the trait
of being the strong silent type; both characters

00:03:31.799 --> 00:03:33.340
rarely up their mouths.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.310
There's one more link that possibly puts these
movies literally in the same universe.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:40.689
One signature of M Night Shyamalan, is that
he almost always makes a cameo appearance

00:03:40.689 --> 00:03:41.680
in his movies.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.860
In Unbreakable, Split, and Glass, he plays
one character named Jai, an IT guy responsible

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:48.680
for setting up security cameras who appears
in all three films.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.120
In The Village, he cameos as the guy reading
the newspaper at the security hut, where his

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.420
name is also Jai.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.750
"Hey Jai, where's the maintenance ladder?"

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:59.469
Maybe this means that all four films take
place in the same universe together.

00:03:59.469 --> 00:04:00.780
Or that he just likes the name Jai.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:02.349
We’ll probably never know for sure.

00:04:02.349 --> 00:04:04.849
One thing we do know that M Night loves is
Philadelphia.

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:08.290
Eleven of his 13 films take place there, and
if you pay close attention, The Village is

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:09.290
one of them.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:12.469
Mr. Walker mentions that he once taught history
at the University of Pennsylvania, which is

00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:13.560
located in Philly.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.579
...and the newspaper seen when Ivy leaves
to woods to retrieve supplies is the Philadelphia

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:18.579
Journal.

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:21.940
The date seen on the newspaper is also the
same as the movie’s release date, July 30th

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:22.940
2004.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:25.970
And if you look at the articles in this newspaper,
they are almost all tragic headlines, which

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:29.530
ties in with the idea of the elders wanting
to escape the horrors of the real world and

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:31.240
start their own society in the woods.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.150
One of the reasons that Ivy’s father is
comfortable with sending her is that she is

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.460
visually blind, and she won’t be able to
convey the secrets of the real world when

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:38.560
she returns.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.990
Even the escorts that set out with her are
instructed not to venture past the road, so

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:45.140
that she would be the only one to go over
the wall and into the outside world.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.931
The wall that she climbs over is covered in
vines of ivy, like all great walls are, and

00:04:48.931 --> 00:04:50.800
her name is literally Ivy Walker.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:52.389
She is the only one to go over the wall.

00:04:52.389 --> 00:04:55.810
It is also fitting that she be the one to
go, because the wildlife preserve that they

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.639
are secretly living on was owned by her grandfather
before his death.

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:02.550
Ivy is blind, but she is able to sense a certain
color that people give off that only her and

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:06.560
her father can sense, and when Lucius is stabbed
by Noah, she is no longer able to sense his

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.520
color because he was dying.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.840
"I cannot see his color."

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.640
You could say that one major theme of the
movie is that it is the things we don’t

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:19.990
see that are most significant.

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:23.360
The villagers don’t see what really lies
beyond their community, the elders don’t

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:27.270
see that they have become the very thing they
ran from and Ivy does not see when she’s

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:30.479
holding the color red, which is used to scare
the people away from ever trying to leave

00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:31.479
the woods.

00:05:31.479 --> 00:05:33.500
This theme is also reflected in the cinematography.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.729
We end up looking away from many of the most
significant events.

00:05:36.729 --> 00:05:38.900
From Ivy and Lucius’s first kiss…

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.720
to Noah’s attack on Lucius…

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.490
To the appearance of Noah in the woods, dressed
as the monster to try to kill Ivy, where our

00:05:55.490 --> 00:06:00.060
view is obstructed and we’re forced to rely
on our other senses to track to the monster.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:08.060
Now if he really had balls, he would have
just made the whole movie black… or maybe

00:06:08.069 --> 00:06:09.069
that’s overkill.

00:06:09.069 --> 00:06:10.289
Or maybe it’s a good podcast idea.

00:06:10.289 --> 00:06:14.569
